# Ticket 03 — DNS Request Timeout

## User Issue

A user reports that websites are not loading.

Testing suggests that DNS requests are timing out rather than returning an answer.

The aim of this investigation was to reproduce a DNS timeout and examine what it looks like in both Windows and Wireshark.

## Creating the Test

I tested DNS resolution using a deliberately non-responsive DNS destination:

nslookup bbc.co.uk 192.0.2.1

Instead of using the computer's normal DNS server, this command instructed nslookup to send the request to 192.0.2.1.

The DNS request timed out.

Windows retried the request several times before eventually displaying:

Request to UnKnown timed-out

![DNS timeout](screenshots/ticket-03-dns-timeout.png)

## Wireshark Investigation

I filtered the capture using:

dns && ip.addr == 192.0.2.1

This isolated the DNS traffic associated with the test.

![DNS timeout Wireshark](screenshots/ticket-03-wireshark-timeout.png)

I identified:

Source:

10.0.2.15

Destination:

192.0.2.1

Query:

bbc.co.uk

Response:

None

The client repeatedly attempted to send the DNS request but no DNS response returned.

This explains why nslookup eventually timed out.

## Troubleshooting a Real Ticket

If a user could reach an external IP address such as 1.1.1.1 but normal DNS requests timed out, I would investigate the DNS configuration.

I could check the configured DNS server using:

ipconfig /all

I could clear the local DNS cache:

ipconfig /flushdns

If appropriate, I could renew the DHCP configuration:

ipconfig /release

ipconfig /renew

I could then retest DNS using:

nslookup bbc.co.uk

If the problem continued, I would investigate whether the configured DNS server was reachable.

On a corporate network I would follow the organisation's procedures rather than changing the computer to a public DNS server, because internal systems may rely on corporate DNS.

If necessary, I would document the troubleshooting already completed and escalate the issue to the network or infrastructure team.

## What I Learned

A DNS timeout is different from an NXDOMAIN response.

With NXDOMAIN, the DNS server responds and tells the client that the requested hostname does not exist.

With a timeout, the client sends the DNS request but receives no response.

Wireshark allowed me to see the repeated DNS queries leaving the computer and confirm that no corresponding responses were returning.

This demonstrated how packet capture can provide additional evidence when standard troubleshooting tools show that a DNS request is timing out.
